diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index af1b59f..acf2bea 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,65 +1,5 @@
-# Barry bags
+# Barry Bags
 
-One clone for every Barry bag that lives outside the `barry` monorepo.
+External bags that live outside the Barry monorepo.
 
-```bash
-git clone --recurse-submodules git@github.com:perintyler/bags.git ~/repos/bags
-cd ~/repos/bags
-./scripts/sync-vendored.sh    # clone the upstream skill repos
-./scripts/check-layout.sh     # verify barry is a sibling and deps are installed
-```
-
-Bags must sit at `~/repos/bags/<name>` with `barry` checked out as a sibling
-at `~/repos/barry` — each bag depends on `@barry/tools` through a relative
-`link:../../barry/packages/tools`, so the layout is load-bearing.
-
-## What is here
-
-**Submodules** — bags we own, each its own `perintyler/<name>-bag` repo.
-They keep independent history and can be cloned or shared alone.
-
-**Vendored** (`scripts/sync-vendored.sh`) — upstream skill repos owned by other
-organizations: `clickhouse-skills`, `temporal-design-skill`,
-`temporal-developer-skill`. Cloned rather than submoduled, because a submodule
-would pin a commit we do not control and turn every upstream bump into a commit
-here. They carry no `bag.yaml` and are not registered as bags.
-
-**Untracked** — anything else in this directory is a local working copy that has
-not been given a repo yet. As of the bags rename every bag here is a submodule;
-`PUBLIC_BAGS.yaml` records which of their repos are public.
-
-## CI
-
-`.github/workflows/ci.yml` installs and typechecks every bag, and runs tests
-for the ones that define them.
-
-It lives here rather than in each bag repo for a practical reason: a bag
-resolves `@barry/tools` through a relative link, so any CI has to check out
-barry beside it. Per-repo workflows would mean 20+ copies each holding a deploy
-key for a private repo. This repo already has every bag as a submodule, so one
-workflow covers them all.
-
-Only the public submodules are built. The default `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot read a
-private submodule, and the one private submodule (`vantage-bags`) holds nested
-bags with no top-level `package.json`, so the build loop skips it anyway. To
-build a private bag later, add a PAT with repo scope as a `BAGS_TOKEN` secret
-and pass it to the checkout step.
-
-## Working on a bag
-
-Each submodule is an ordinary repo. Commit and push inside it as usual; the
-pointer in this repo only moves when you commit the updated submodule here:
-
-```bash
-cd linear && git commit -am "..." && git push
-cd .. && git add linear && git commit -m "bump linear"
-```
-
-Forgetting that second step is the usual submodule papercut — the bag is
-pushed, but this repo still points at the old commit.
-
-## After cloning
-
-`pnpm install` inside each bag. A bag with no `node_modules` fails its build
-loudly (`Could not resolve "@barry/tools"`) rather than silently resolving
-through the monorepo.
+See the [Barry bags docs](https://github.com/perintyler/barry/blob/master/docs/bags.md).
